# Task Records

Create a task record for significant, risky, or multi-session work. A task
records scope and execution status; it does not authorize the work.

Each task identifies authority basis, owner, dependencies, in/out scope,
acceptance criteria, affected artifacts, side effects, validation, evidence,
review, rollback, blockers, and next action.

`dependencies` contains only hard `TASK-####` prerequisites. A task may become
`ready`, resume after `blocked`, or resume after `reopened` only when every
dependency is `completed`, every referenced gate is `passed` or `waived`, all
structured blockers are resolved, and reciprocal plan links are consistent.
Dates record provenance, freshness, expiry, or an external constraint; they do
not make work ready or choose its sequence.

Run `./octon work resume` and inspect its derived ready frontier before selecting
planned work. The frontier identifies eligible work but grants no permission.
When several tasks are eligible, use current operator direction or an accepted
priority/value/risk decision rather than a date-based inference.

When an adopted project enables governed work completion, `./octon work close`
may also record an optional `work_completion` contract: exact staging paths,
commit message, optional PR text and self-review references, plus the shared
base, declared write scope, worktree owner, handback, partial-result, and
coordination evidence required by `concurrent_work`. This contract makes a
later read-only completion plan deterministic. It is not authorization to
stage, commit, publish, review, integrate, synchronize, or delete anything.
Older task records without this optional field remain valid; they simply
cannot be used by `./octon work finish` until explicitly migrated or superseded.
Reopening a completed task removes its prior completion contract so any later
closure must record a fresh exact change inventory and commit/review inputs.
